NAME
    vectors - where each interrupt vector points

SYNOPSIS
    vectors

DESCRIPTION
    Reads the interrupt vector table out of the running machine and shows
    what each entry points at, resolved through the label registry. 'Which
    routine handles NMI' is a question that comes up in almost every
    investigation, and the alternative is remembering that the SNES keeps
    its native NMI vector at $00FFEA and hand-reading two bytes.

    Vectors are grouped by CPU mode, because a 65816 has two full sets and
    which one the hardware uses depends on the E flag at the moment the
    interrupt is taken. 'regs' shows E. A game that sets up only the native
    table and then takes an interrupt in emulation mode jumps somewhere it
    never intended, and seeing both tables side by side is what makes that
    visible.

    When coverage recording is on ('man cov'), a vector whose target has
    actually executed is marked '(ran)'. A vector that points at plausible
    code which never ran is a much stronger signal than one that merely
    looks wrong - it says the interrupt is not firing at all, rather than
    firing and doing the wrong thing.

    The pointers are read live through the CpuBus, so a game that rewrites
    its own vector table (or maps different ROM in) reports what is mapped
    right now, not what was there at reset.

EXAMPLES
    vectors
    cov on; vectors

SEE ALSO
    runto, bt, cov, label, addr
